## Embedded subprocesses and boundary handlers

Use an ordinary embedded subprocess to group an activity's internal workflow:
{"type":"subProcess","id":"review","label":"Review application","expanded":true,
 "process":[{"type":"startEvent","id":"review_start"},
            {"type":"userTask","id":"verify","label":"Verify documents"},
            {"type":"endEvent","id":"review_end"}]}

The `process` array is a separate scope with exactly one NONE start event and at
least one end event. Subprocesses can contain gateways, tasks, annotations, further
embedded subprocesses, and boundary handlers. IDs must be unique across the entire
diagram. Flows and branch `next` references must stay inside their scope. Set
`expanded` false to display a collapsed subprocess while preserving its contents.
Assign the subprocess itself to a pool lane; do not assign its internal nodes to
parent lanes. Call activities, event subprocesses, transactions, subprocess loop
markers, and internal lane sets are not supported yet.

Tasks and subprocesses may have a `boundary_events` array. The host is implicit:
never insert a boundary event into the normal process sequence and never put its
handler inside the subprocess's `process` unless it attaches to an internal task.

Example: add this field to an approval task for a non-interrupting reminder:
"boundary_events":[{
  "type":"boundaryEvent","id":"approval_timeout","label":"48 hours",
  "eventDefinition":"timerEventDefinition","cancel_activity":false,
  "timer":{"type":"duration","value":"PT48H"},
  "path":[{"type":"sendTask","id":"remind","label":"Send reminder"},
          {"type":"endEvent","id":"reminder_end"}]
}]

Use `cancel_activity`: true (default) to interrupt the host and follow the exception
path, or false for a timer reminder that runs alongside the continuing activity.
Error boundary events use `eventDefinition`: "errorEventDefinition" and MUST
interrupt (`cancel_activity`: true). They may carry `event_reference` with an id,
optional name and code matching an error end event inside the subprocess. Omit the
reference for a catch-all error handler. Error handlers must not carry `timer`.

Each boundary `path` contains normal elements, without a start event. Finish with
an end event or set boundary `next` to an existing node in the SAME scope to rejoin
the main path. An empty `path` requires `next`. Do not implicitly continue the host's
normal sequence after the handler. A boundary inherits its host's lane; assign
handler activities/events to appropriate lanes when the enclosing pool uses lanes.

Timer boundary events require `timer`: {"type":"duration"|"date"|"cycle","value":"..."}.
Use ISO 8601 values such as PT48H, 2026-12-01T09:00:00Z, or R3/PT24H, or preserve
an existing expression. The same timer field is available on timer start/catch
events. Never replace an existing timer expression with a label alone.

For ANY edit to a diagram containing subprocesses or boundary handlers (including
adding the first one), use `replace_process` with the complete updated process.
Preserve unchanged IDs, subprocess contents, timers, handlers, and expansion flags.
